+//! The **`orphan:` ledger value** — the evidence an unreference leaves over one fragment
+//! position, so the custodian GC reclaims the bytes there only after a reader-safe grace window
+//! (proposal 0016 decision 4.2,
+//! `docs/design/proposals/draft/0016-multipart-commit-protocol.md:1121-1408`).
+//!
+//! The key is [`crate::metadata::orphan_key`] (`orphan:<dserver>:<chunk>:<index>`); this module is
+//! the value under it. It has **three shapes**, and every one decodes, forever
+//! (`0016:1190-1216`, `:1321-1333`):
+//!
+//! | Shape | Spelling | Written by |
+//! |---|---|---|
+//! | legacy | the bare canonical decimal `orphaned_at_millis` | every writer in the tree before this module: a delete, an overwrite, a completed repair or rebalance, the post-restore pass |
+//! | evidence | `{"orphaned_at_millis":N,"event":"<event>"}` | a writer that knows **which unreference event** the mark evidences ([`MarkEvent`]) |
+//! | reclaiming | `{"orphaned_at_millis":N[,"event":"<event>"],"reclaiming":true}` | the GC pass, as its **reclamation intent**, committed before it destroys the bytes |
+//!
+//! A legacy mark carries no identity at all, so it is read as the `evidence` shape's
+//! "different identity" arm by any unreference event that meets it (`0016:1202-1207`). A
+//! `reclaiming` value keeps the stamp and the identity it replaced — the grace test that licensed
+//! it already passed on that stamp, and a restart resumes the reclamation rather than re-judging
+//! it (`0016:1321-1333`).
+//!
+//! # Structural validity at decode, and one spelling per value (ADR-0045)
+//!
+//! [`decode_orphan_mark`] accepts **exactly** [`OrphanMark::encode`]'s image, byte for byte: a
+//! legacy stamp with a sign or a leading zero, a structured value with its fields reordered or
+//! whitespace inserted, `"reclaiming":false` spelled out, `"event":null`, or an identity-free
+//! structured value (whose only spelling is the bare decimal) are all refused. The reason is the
+//! one the multipart records give (`crate::multipart`, "Serialization identity"): every writer of
+//! an existing mark CASes it on its **exact bytes** (`require(orphan:<pos> == prior)`,
+//! `0016:677-680`), so a second spelling of one value is a mark no CAS can match.
+//!
+//! A value that does not decode is **never** rewritten by a maintenance pass: it is left exactly
+//! as it is, and the pass that met it names it for a human (ADR-0045 decision 3).
+
+use std::fmt;
+
+use bytes::Bytes;
+use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
+use wyrd_traits::{ChunkId, DServerId};
+
+use crate::metadata;
+use crate::multipart::{self, RecordError, RetireMode, RetireToken};
+
+/// The unreference event an `orphan:` mark evidences — the identity the three-arm rule of
+/// `0016:1218-1224` compares: a writer that finds a mark carrying its **own** event skips it
+/// (a duplicate of this retirement), one carrying a **different** event — or a legacy mark, which
+/// carries none — replaces it with a fresh stamp and its own identity.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub enum MarkEvent {
+    /// A **retirement** — a drained obligation, an owned-`sidx:` walk (`s:<upload-id>:<epoch>`),
+    /// a superseded generation (`g:<inode>:<version>`). Its token **is** the tail of its
+    /// obligation's key, so "is this retirement still draining?" is one keyed read of
+    /// [`MarkEvent::retirement_key`] — never an expansion of the obligation (`0016:1242-1247`).
+    Retirement(RetireToken),
+    /// A **placement move** — a repoint's or a staged re-place's destination pre-mark and the
+    /// evidence it leaves on the position it vacates. Identified per move, never by the
+    /// `{inode, version}` or session epoch the move operates on, which successive moves of one
+    /// chunk share (`0016:1177-1187`).
