+//! The **staged reference build** — proposal 0016 decision 2
+//! (`docs/design/proposals/draft/0016-multipart-commit-protocol.md:765-891`): the fragments a
+//! multipart upload has made durable but not yet published, as one set every maintenance pass
+//! reads beside the committed placements.
+//!
+//! Two sources, both read through **bounded per-session ranges**, never a global scan
+//! (`0016:770-774`, `:890`): for every session record in the store (`mpu:`, a scan the admission
+//! counter bounds at `MAX_SESSIONS`), its **in-flight owned entries** (`sidx:<id>:`, each carrying
+//! its chunk's planned placement) and its **committed parts** (`part:<id>:`, each chunk with its
+//! committed placement and EC scheme).
+//!
+//! **Source before destination (normative, `0016:782-800`).** A part commit atomically deletes a
+//! chunk's `sidx:` entries and writes the `part:` record that protects the same bytes, so each
+//! session's `sidx:` range is read **before** its `part:` range: an interleaving then observes the
+//! chunk in both classes or in `part:` alone, never in neither. The caller extends the same order
+//! one handoff further, reading this set before the committed inodes a publication moves the
+//! chunks into ([`crate::gc::referenced_fragments`]).
+//!
+//! **Every session that exists, in any state.** 0016 names the in-flight entries of `Open`
+//! sessions; this build reads every session's, because an entry outliving its session's `Open`
+//! state is residue the teardown walk orphan-marks and deletes in one batch (`0016:2197-2201`), so
+//! until that walk reaches it nothing else evidences it. Protecting it costs a drain nothing it
+//! would not wait for anyway, and it keeps the answer free of a read of the session's state that
+//! could be stale by the time the range is read.
+//!
+//! **What a record this build cannot read costs.** A `part:` record or owned entry that will not
+//! decode, or a session key that will not parse, hides which fragments it names, so the reference
+//! set is **incomplete** — the same containment an unreadable committed map gets (recorded in the
+//! caller's `unresolvable`): no pass reclaims anything, none certifies. A session **record** that
+//! will not decode is different: its key still names both of its ranges, so its fragments are
+//! read and protected like any other session's, and it is named on the side — only its **state**
+//! is unknown, which is what the restore fence and a staged re-place need
+//! ([`StagedSet::unreadable_sessions`]).
