+### What protects a durable byte, and what may reclaim it
+
+Every durable byte is, at every instant, in at least one of three classes: **committed-referenced** (a committed chunk map places it), **staged** (a live upload's records name it — a committed part's `part:` record, or the owned `sidx:` entry of a part still streaming), or **evidenced garbage** (an `orphan:` mark says since when nothing references it, and the reclaimer waits out a reader-safe grace window from then). The claim is *no gaps*, not a partition: protection deliberately **overlaps** across every handoff — a part commit moves a chunk from its owned entry to its part record in one batch, a publication moves it from the part record to the committed map, a retirement marks bytes before it deletes the record that protected them — so a reader of two classes reads the source before the destination and sees each chunk in at least one of them (proposal 0016 decision 2).
+
+The custodian's reference set carries the staged class as a second member **beside** the committed placements, never merged into them, so each maintenance pass makes its own decision about it. GC and the post-restore pass protect both staged kinds exactly as they protect committed content; a drain counts both as held, so a server whose only bytes belong to an upload still streaming is never reported safe to decommission (the wait is bounded, since every staged byte leaves within the upload's residency ceiling); scrub verifies a committed part's fragments against the scheme its part record carries and queues repair for a corrupt one (an in-flight chunk has no committed scheme yet); reconstruction rebuilds a lost staged fragment and repoints its part record only under the upload's fence — pre-marking the destination before writing a byte there, then one compare-and-set on the session record, the part record and that pre-mark — so a Complete or Abort racing the repair wins cleanly and the pre-written fragment stays evidenced for GC; rebalance leaves staged bytes where they are. A record the build cannot read hides which fragments it names, so it makes the set incomplete, and an incomplete set reclaims and certifies nothing. The post-restore pass also **fences** every upload a restored image resurrected, because an image cannot prove the bytes its staging records name still exist, and records that in the `restore:fence` generation (section 5).
+
+GC reads the `orphan:` ledger in **bounded pages**, a fixed number per pass, and keeps its place between passes in the store (`custodian:gc:orphan-walk`), so a ledger larger than any one read still drains and its tail is never starved behind its head. A mark takes one of three shapes, all readable forever: the bare timestamp every writer before these passes wrote, `{orphaned_at_millis, event}` naming the unreference event that wrote it, and that marked `reclaiming`; a value in none of them is left untouched and named for a human, never rewritten. GC records a reclamation as the `reclaiming` value **before** it deletes the bytes and resumes one a crash interrupted; it treats a mark whose retirement is still pending as protecting, by one keyed read of that retirement, never by expanding it; and it deletes a mark with no fragment beneath it only once no write it evidences can still land there and the absence was observed after that point. Until a clean sweep of the whole ledger has dropped the stale marks an older build left on still-referenced fragments — recorded durably as `custodian:gc:orphan-identity`, and withdrawn by a restore — GC reclaims nothing on any mark.
+
